  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The right tools for owner builders are not the same as a framer's truck full of saws. When you are acting as your own general contractor, your job is to measure, verify, inspect, document and keep people safe, so your kit should make those jobs fast and accurate. Here is what is worth buying, what is optional, and how each tool earns its keep on a Texas job site.</p>

<p>If you are still deciding whether to run the job yourself, start with our guide on <a href="/how-to-be-your-own-general-contractor/">how to be your own general contractor</a>. The tool list below assumes you have hired licensed trades and are managing them, not swinging a hammer eight hours a day.</p>

<h2>Measuring and verification: catch mistakes before they are built in</h2>

<p>Most costly errors on a new home are not dramatic. They are a window rough opening that is two inches off, a wall that drifted from the plan, a slab that is out of level, or a cabinet run that will not fit because a soffit came down too low. You catch these by measuring early and often, and by checking the work against your plans before the next trade covers it.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Laser measure.</strong> Your most-used tool. Use it to check room dimensions, ceiling heights, window and door openings and cabinet walls against the drawings during each site walk. A model with Bluetooth that saves readings to your phone makes it easy to build a record.</li>
<li><strong>Tape measure.</strong> Still essential for short runs, outlet heights, rough openings and anything a laser cannot reach cleanly. Get a 25-foot tape with a wide, stiff blade.</li>
<li><strong>Level.</strong> A 4-foot level lets you spot-check framing, door jambs, window sills and countertops. You are not setting forms; you are confirming that what was built is plumb and level before you sign off on a draw.</li>
</ul>

<!--products:laser_measure,tape_measure,level|Measuring tools for checking work against your plans-->

<p>Make it a habit to walk the house with the plans and a laser after every major phase. Note discrepancies in writing the same day and send them to the responsible sub. Fixing a framing error costs a little; fixing it after drywall and paint costs a lot.</p>

<h2>Inspection tools: see what the city inspector might not</h2>

<p>The city or county inspector checks for code compliance, and that matters. But code is a minimum, and inspectors have limited time per house. A few inexpensive inspection tools let you look closer at moisture, electrical work and insulation, especially at the pre-drywall and final stages. Our <a href="/new-construction-inspection-checklist/">new construction inspection checklist</a> walks through exactly what to look for at each phase.</p>

<h3>Moisture meter</h3>
<p>Framing lumber that gets rained on, which happens on plenty of Texas builds, needs time to dry before it is closed in. A [[amz:moisture_meter|pin-type moisture meter]] lets you check stud and plate moisture before insulation and drywall go up. Check for wet subfloor around windows and the slab edges too. If readings are high, ask your framer and drywall sub how they want to handle it before you close the walls.</p>

<h3>Outlet tester</h3>
<p>Once power is on, a plug-in outlet tester takes seconds per receptacle and flags common wiring faults like open grounds or reversed polarity. Testers with a GFCI test button help confirm GFCI protection in kitchens, baths, garages and outdoors. This does not replace your electrician or the electrical inspection; it simply helps you build an accurate punch list.</p>

<h3>IR thermometer and thermal camera</h3>
<p>An IR thermometer gives you spot temperatures: supply and return air at HVAC registers, surface temps at windows, or a breaker that is running warm. A thermal camera goes further by showing a picture of temperature differences across a wall or ceiling, which makes missing insulation, air leaks and hidden moisture much easier to see. In a Texas summer, the difference between a well-insulated attic hatch and a poorly sealed one shows up clearly on screen.</p>

<h3>Headlamp</h3>
<p>Attics, crawl spaces, closets with no fixtures yet and the inside of wall cavities are dark. A bright headlamp keeps both hands free for your phone, meter or tape. It is the cheapest tool on this list and one you will use every walk.</p>

<!--products:moisture_meter,outlet_tester,thermal_camera,headlamp|Inspection gear for pre-drywall and final walkthroughs-->

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Do your own pre-drywall walk with a headlamp, moisture meter and your phone camera the day before insulation goes in. That window is the last chance to see plumbing, wiring, blocking and framing without cutting into finished walls.</p></div>

<h2>Site safety: protect yourself and your visitors</h2>

<p>As an owner-builder you are on site often, and so are family members, lenders, inspectors and suppliers. A job site is full of tripping hazards, nails, open floor penetrations and overhead work. Basic safety gear is non-negotiable.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Hard hat.</strong> Wear one whenever anyone is working overhead, during framing and roofing especially. Keep a spare or two for visitors.</li>
<li><strong>Safety glasses.</strong> Dust, debris and flying fragments are constant during framing, drywall and trim. Clear, anti-fog glasses are worth it in Texas humidity.</li>
<li><strong>First aid kit.</strong> Keep a stocked kit in your truck or the site trailer. Cuts and splinters are common; know the address of the nearest urgent care before you need it.</li>
</ul>

<p>Also confirm that every sub carries their own insurance and follows their own safety program. Talk to your insurance agent about builder's risk and liability coverage for an owner-builder project. Our post on <a href="/how-to-hire-and-manage-subcontractors/">hiring and managing subcontractors</a> covers the paperwork to collect before anyone starts work.</p>

<h2>Site security: protect materials and appliances</h2>

<p>Theft of lumber, copper, tools and appliances is a real concern on new construction, especially in growing suburbs where many homes are going up at once. A [[amz:site_camera|cellular or solar-powered site camera]] can deter theft, alert you to activity after hours, and give you a time-stamped record of deliveries and crew arrival times. That record can be helpful when you are reconciling invoices or settling a dispute about when work was done.</p>

<p>Pair the camera with common-sense steps: schedule appliance delivery as close to move-in as possible, lock the house once doors are hung, and avoid leaving high-value materials stacked in the open overnight. Post signage that the site is monitored, and check local rules about recording.</p>

<h2>Paperwork: the owner-builder's real job</h2>

<p>Here is the truth about owner-building: much of your work happens on paper. Bids, contracts, lien waivers, change orders, inspection results, draw requests, warranties and receipts pile up fast. Losing track of them can cost you money with your lender, your subs and later your insurance company.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>House-building planner.</strong> A [[amz:home_planner|home-building planner journal]] gives you a structured place for budgets, schedules, contacts and selections, so nothing lives only in your head.</li>
<li><strong>Document organizer.</strong> An accordion file or portable organizer keeps signed contracts, permits, lien waivers and warranties together and ready to grab for a lender draw meeting.</li>
<li><strong>Graph paper notebook.</strong> Handy for sketching field changes, outlet locations, furniture layouts and quick notes during a site walk. A sketch with dimensions is often clearer to a sub than a long text.</li>
</ul>

<!--products:home_planner,document_organizer,graph_paper_notebook|Planning and paperwork tools for staying organized-->

<p>Back up the paper with photos. Shoot every wall before drywall, label photos by room, and store them in a shared folder. If you are planning your budget categories now, our <a href="/budgeting-for-your-texas-home-build-tips-for-staying-on-track/">Texas home build budgeting guide</a> pairs well with a planner.</p>

<h2>Light DIY: a few basics for small jobs</h2>

<p>Even if you hire out every trade, you will end up doing small tasks: hanging a temporary sign, fixing a loose stair rail on the dumpster ramp, installing closet shelving, mounting a mailbox or putting up a few smoke detectors after final. A compact drill and impact driver combo covers most of that. Add a sturdy tool bag to keep your measuring and inspection gear together so it rides in the truck and is ready for every walk.</p>

<p>Be honest with yourself about scope. Taking on a trade you are not licensed or experienced in, such as electrical or gas, can create safety, inspection and insurance problems. Check your local rules on what a homeowner may do on their own permit before you pick up the tools.</p>

<h2>A simple owner-builder kit, by phase</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Tools you will reach for most</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Lot and foundation</td><td>Laser measure, tape, level, hard hat, planner</td></tr>
<tr><td>Framing</td><td>Laser measure, level, moisture meter, hard hat, safety glasses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rough-in and pre-drywall</td><td>Headlamp, moisture meter, phone camera, graph paper notebook</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insulation and drywall</td><td>Thermal camera or IR thermometer, headlamp</td></tr>
<tr><td>Final and punch list</td><td>Outlet tester, IR thermometer, level, tape, document organizer</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>You do not need everything on day one. Start with measuring tools, safety gear and a planner, and add inspection tools as you approach rough-in. For a full list of what happens when, see our <a href="/new-home-construction-timeline/">new home construction timeline</a>.</p>

